  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front this evening will bring scattered \nshowers and cooler temperatures for tonight and into the weekend.\n\nA cold front will push through the area this evening, and will \nbe offshore and south of the forecast area before midnight. \nScattered showers and perhaps a few rumbles of thunder are \nexpected to accompany the frontal passage. PoPs are mainly in \nthe 50-60% range area wide, and were adjusted to match closer to\nthe latest guidance's timing of the frontal progression.\n\nSome anafrontal showers and/or sprinkles will likely linger \novernight and perhaps into the first half of Saturday, mainly \nsouth of the I-95 corridor to as far north as Philly. Event \ntotal rainfall through Saturday will not amount to much, mainly \naround 0.10-0.20\" at most. Saturday afternoon should be mainly \ndry area wide with decreasing cloud cover, except for southern \nDelaware and Cape May.\n\nHigh pressure will build into the region from the north during \nSaturday. Mainly cloudy skies along and southeast of I-95 early\nwill given way to clearing skies by afternoon for most of the \narea. Mostly cloudy skies will likely remain the theme all day \nacross Delmarva and extreme southern New Jersey though, where\nthe showers may linger into the early afternoon. Seasonable, \nbut slightly below normal temperatures expected with highs in \nthe upper 60s to low 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers are expected to impact portions of the \nregion Sunday and Sunday night.\n\nLow pressure developing along the stalled frontal boundary to\nour south Sunday and Sunday night will provide another round of\nshowers across portions of the region. The timing of the showers\nwill be focused during the afternoon hours Sunday, but could\nbegin near/south of Philly in the morning and linger area wide\ninto the evening or overnight hours. PoPs have increased some,\nand now range from around 30-40% along and north of I-80 to\naround 50-70% within the I-95 corridor and Philly metro, to\n70-90% across Delmarva and far southern New Jersey. The latest\nQPF forecast ranges from around 0.10\" in the far north, to about\n0.25\" within the I-95 corridor and Philly metro, to 0.50-1\" \nacross Delmarva and far southern New Jersey. It's possible the\nshowers don't make it much farther northwest than the I-95\ncorridor. There still remains spread in the guidance as to\nexactly how far inland the showers will make it.\n\nGiven overcast skies, easterly winds, and showers, temperatures\nSunday will be cooler with highs likely only reaching the low to\nmid 60s in many areas.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Canadian high pressure will bring much cooler \ntemperatures during the first half of next week, including some\npotential for frost near and north of I-78 Tuesday night.\n\nCanadian high pressure and a much cooler air mass will then \nbuild into the region starting Monday into Tuesday. Monday will\nremain mild with highs within a few degrees of 70, though a\nnorthwest breeze will make it feel cooler. Some lingering clouds\nearly should give way to mostly sunny skies later in the day.\nLows mainly in the 40s to low 50s Monday night.\n\nHigh temperatures Tuesday are only in the low 60s for much of \nthe region, which is upwards of 10 degrees below normal. Tuesday\nwill feature clear and sunny skies though. Some areas of frost \nare possible by Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, especially\nnorth of I-78. Forecast lows are in the mid 30s north of I-78, \nand in the low to mid 40s elsewhere.\n\nAs cold advection wanes by midweek, the air mass will begin to\nmodify and trend notably warmer by Thursday and into Friday. \nHighs will trend closer to normal near 70 degrees.",
